#N canvas 20 20 1500 1120 12;
#X text 20 20 display-diag -- load this ALONGSIDE mother.pd and main.pd to get a real console for the display arbiter. It does not touch the deployed patch and it draws nothing. See ref/conventions.md \, There IS a console., f 58;
#X text 20 110 IT COUNTS RATHER THAN DUMPS. The home frame is ten OSC messages at 10 Hz \; printing every one of them over SSH would slow down the thing being measured. FRAMES and MESSAGES are cumulative totals printed once a second \, so the per-second rate is the difference between consecutive lines -- FRAMES should climb by 10 and MESSAGES by about 100., f 58;
#X obj 20 250 r disp;
#X obj 20 290 print DISP;
#X obj 300 250 r err;
#X obj 300 290 print ERR;
#X obj 20 380 r oscOut;
#X obj 20 420 t a a;
#X obj 300 470 route sendtyped;
#X obj 300 510 route /oled/gFlip;
#X obj 300 550 t b;
#X obj 760 470 t b;
#X obj 300 610 f;
#X obj 440 650 + 1;
#X obj 300 690 t f f;
#X obj 560 750 f;
#X obj 560 800 print FRAMES;
#X obj 760 610 f;
#X obj 900 650 + 1;
#X obj 760 690 t f f;
#X obj 1020 750 f;
#X obj 1020 800 print MESSAGES;
#X obj 20 690 loadbang;
#X msg 20 730 1;
#X obj 20 770 metro 1000;
#X obj 20 820 t b b;
#X text 20 900 The snapshot f on each counter is read by the metro and written by the counter \, so reading never disturbs the count. Both are cumulative on purpose -- a rate that is dropping shows up as the gap between lines shrinking \, which is easier to see than a noisy per-second figure., f 46;
#X text 420 900 DISP and ERR are printed in full because they are slow: a knob turn is a few messages a second and an error is rare. Those are the buses where you want the exact message \, traps and all., f 46;
#X text 820 900 Deploy first \, then run this by hand as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd \, so oscOut \, disp and err are all live., f 46;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
